  "questions": {
    "literal": "Python has an expression literal() that writes a value into a statement's SQL, and reads a pipeline's plain operand as a literal (null() is literal(None)); JavaScript has no statement literal and binds a pipeline's plain operand. Both pipelines have literal().",
    "shapes": "One feature offered through differently shaped API: grouping elements, table functions, conflict arbiters, MERGE actions, LIKE patterns, the identifier wrapper, model writes, registered inserts, a model or registered column's helpers and a pipeline's own terminals.",
    "raw-sql": "Python runs raw SQL as RawSQL(template, values), with inline_sql(); JavaScript's terminals take SQL text and a parameter array.",
    "records": "Python's Record carries per-column Field metadata and gives one column tagged with tagged(name); JavaScript's rows are plain objects with no field metadata, tagged per result with { tagged: true }.",
    "capabilities": "Python publishes a capability manifest (capabilities(), require_capability(), UnsupportedCapabilityError) and Value.snapshot() for the generated campaign; JavaScript lists registrations with entities(), graphs() and sourceTuples().",
    "update-many-guard": "JavaScript's registered update_many() needs where or all_rows() before it runs; Python's updates every row when it has no filter.",
    "schema-api": "Python spells DDL as one function per ALTER TABLE, enum and ALTER DOMAIN action and builds views with keyword arguments; JavaScript builds them with typestate builders and pending ALTER objects."
  },
